Evaluating the Keplerian-integral linkage against real astrometry.

The question these answer is not "does fo get the right elements" but "does
fo account for the arc it was given".  Element agreement hides the failure
that matters:  on 1999 EO3, with 33 observations in 1999 and 283 in 2020-21,
fo drops the 1999 apparition entirely and fits the recent one alone, yet
still lands within 0.06% of MPCORB in a, because the recent apparition is by
itself well observed.  The dropped apparition is visible only in the fitted
arc.

Pipeline, in order.  Paths to MPCORB.DAT and UnnObs.txt are hard-coded at the
top of each script.

  parse_mpcorb.py   > unn.tsv           unnumbered orbits, for reference
                                        elements to score against
  index_unnobs.py   > unnobs_index.tsv  one row per object describing the
                                        observations that are actually there

MPCORB cannot be used to choose the sample.  It is generated later than
UnnObs.txt and knows about linkages the observation file does not:  2013 HA62
is listed with 16 observations over 2013-2025, but UnnObs.txt holds only the
eight from 2013.  Selecting on MPCORB's arc therefore mis-describes the input
fo is given, so the strata come from the index instead.

  select2.py N SEED > sample.tsv        stratified draw, biased toward
                                        apparitions far apart, split by
                                        inclination in the hard strata
  extract2.py sample.tsv obsdir         one pass over UnnObs.txt (~30 s),
                                        writing <desig>.obs per object
  runner.py sample.tsv obsdir outdir RUNS WORKERS [base,ki]
                                        runs fo both ways, several times, one
                                        working and config directory per job
  arcfrac.py sample.tsv outdir/runs.tsv     arc recovered, per stratum
  elemcheck.py sample.tsv outdir/runs.tsv   whether the recovered arcs give
                                            the right orbit

Run elemcheck.py, not just arcfrac.py.  A linkage joining two apparitions
that do not belong together recovers the arc exactly as well as a correct
one:  an early version of this work gained 29 objects on the arc metric and
had 19 of them wrong, which arcfrac.py alone reports as a clear win.

Three ways fo will silently invalidate a comparison, all handled in
runner.py and all of which produced a wrong answer here first:

  - Settings persist into environ.dat, and reads try ~/.find_orb before
    honouring -x, so one stray run with KEPLERIAN_LINK=1 enables it for
    every later run including the controls.  Pass the setting explicitly in
    both arms;  never rely on its absence.
  - -x needs a trailing slash, or files land beside the directory rather
    than in it.
  - fo starts from a stored solution unless given -i, and seeds statistical
    ranging from rand(), so about 5% of objects differ run to run.  Measure
    that noise floor before believing any difference.

Also:  fo asserts at fo.cpp:271 on partial files that were never created
(-k skips it), and the input file must be argv[1], with switches after it.
